**Read:** [Non-ferrous Extractive Metallurgy — Ray and Pugazhenthy, NPTEL](../sources/Nonferrous-Extractive-Metallurgy-NPTEL.lingua#r_29G1ZF58Y8EG123MNX3ZRNRE2W), all zinc, lead, tin, oxide/sulfide, refining, and secondary-production material; [Non-ferrous Metals Industries — European Commission BREF](../sources/Nonferrous-Metals-Industries-BREF.lingua#r_19ZD7KKDWVWM20MXVNCQZJMCDB), whole corresponding metal-family chapters and shared gas/water/residual methods; [Materials, energy, and heat balances in metallurgical processes — Satish Ch. Koria](../sources/Metallurgical-Material-and-Heat-Balances-NPTEL.lingua#r_38NWQRDJH3RVM3NNGB71RR1X43), the complete lead, zinc/imperial-smelting, and relevant heat-balance exercises; [Metais Não Ferrosos e suas Ligas — Cassio Barbosa](../sources/Metais-Nao-Ferrosos-e-Suas-Ligas-Barbosa.lingua#r_0FJ1378X0JMVHYVBFAXGK2399D), whole relevant metal/alloy chapters, when available.

Different metals distribute among condensed and gas phases differently under the actual chemical/thermal conditions. Zinc, lead, and tin feed families illustrate why oxidation/reduction, volatility, solution chemistry, physical separation, and impurity behavior need explicit models instead of one generic metal-extraction diagram.

Compare complete thermal and solution/electrochemical routes on their defined products and feeds. Condensed fumes/dust, slag, residues, electrolyte inventories, and associated elements can carry significant target or hazardous constituents. Collection and internal returns can improve recovery while accumulating an impurity elsewhere.

Keep material identity, workplace/environmental measurements, containment/capture, residual treatment, maintenance, and product specification connected. A useful engineering study uses the actual professional method and measurement context, distinguishing measured results from an assumed universal concentration or exposure criterion.

**Work:** reconstruct a complete zinc route and a complete lead or tin route from full technical sources. Independently balance target and associated impurities across metal, gas/dust, slag/residue, solution, and returns. Derive a representative volatility, reduction, or solution-separation relation and compare it with observations. Evaluate an alternative process/quality arrangement and explain the continuing recovery/control work required by its residual streams.

**Continue:** [Connect reactive-metal production with compound preparation, reduction, and contamination evidence](Titanium-magnesium-reactive-metals-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_5EDSESE8BJNK0ZJXE74BRF493P).

